-- Listening Lesson 10 step by step (IELTS-10 v0.3.0), steps 1-3 of 6: five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer, the transcript only after submitting). Audio is cut
-- from the owner-approved lesson recording (no new speech) and stored in the private ielts-targeted-listening bucket
-- by exact SHA-256. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 15:17 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'. Named-student pilot with
-- requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_LISTENING_06.json.
do $steps$
declare tA constant text := 'LECTURER: Good morning, everyone. In today''s lecture I want to look at something we all experience but rarely think about: waiting in queues. Businesses spend a surprising amount of effort designing how we wait, and many of the principles behind these designs go back to a well-known paper on the psychology of waiting lines, published in the nineteen-eighties.
LECTURER: Let''s start with the shape of the queue itself. Many banks and post offices once had a separate line for each counter. The problem was that customers kept comparing their own line with the others, and they became frustrated whenever another line moved faster. A single line feeding several counters solves this. The average wait may be almost the same, but people consider the system fairer, and that sense of fairness makes the wait easier to accept.
LECTURER: This leads to an important point. What matters to customers is often not the actual length of the wait, but how long it feels. Last term I tried an informal poll with one of my seminar groups. I asked them to guess how long they had queued in the canteen that day, and then we compared their guesses with the times on their receipts. I''d predicted that the guesses would be about fifty per cent too long, but in fact most people guessed roughly double their real wait. That was one small group, so treat it as an illustration rather than a research finding, but it does show how unreliable our sense of waiting time can be. So the question for designers is what makes a wait feel longer than it really is, and several factors stand out.
LECTURER: The first is having nothing to do. Unoccupied time drags. A well-known example comes from office buildings where people complained that the lifts were too slow. Rather than install faster lifts, which would have been expensive, managers put mirrors beside the lift doors. People spent the time checking their appearance, and the complaints largely stopped, even though the lifts were no faster.
LECTURER: The second factor is uncertainty. If you don''t know how long you''ll be waiting, every minute seems longer. That''s why many theme parks and call centres now display or announce an estimated waiting time. Interestingly, some organisations deliberately make that estimate a little longer than they expect the wait to be, so that customers are pleasantly surprised when they''re served early.'; tB constant text := 'LECTURER: Third, unexplained waits feel longer than explained ones. Passengers on a delayed train are far more patient when the driver gives the reason for the delay, even if the news itself is bad. Silence, on the other hand, tends to make people imagine the worst.
LECTURER: The fourth factor concerns the stage of the process. Waiting before a service begins feels longer than waiting once it has started. Restaurants understand this very well. Rather than leaving customers standing by the door until a table is ready, they often hand them a menu, because once you''re choosing your meal, you feel the experience has already begun.
LECTURER: A further factor, and one that is easy to overlook, is company. People who wait alone consistently report the time as longer than people who wait with friends or family. Conversation is a natural distraction, and it can work even between strangers, which is why some waiting areas now arrange their seating so that people can talk more easily.
LECTURER: The last factor, and perhaps the most powerful, is fairness. People will tolerate a long wait if everyone is treated equally, but even a short wait becomes unbearable if someone pushes in. Seeing someone jump the queue produces anger out of all proportion to the time actually lost.
LECTURER: Finally, let''s think about the future of queuing. Increasingly, the line itself is disappearing. In virtual queues, customers register when they arrive and are then told when it''s their turn. Early systems handed out electronic pagers, but so many of these went missing that most venues now simply send a message to the customer''s own phone. This allows people to shop, eat or simply sit down instead of standing in line. It doesn''t remove the wait, of course, but it changes how the wait feels, which, as we''ve seen, is what really matters.
LECTURER: In the next lecture, we''ll look at how these ideas have been applied in hospitals, where waiting can be a matter of health rather than convenience.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,audio_path,audio_sha256,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-listening-l15-v1','0.1.0','Listening Lesson 10 step 1 of 6: Follow the signposts','listening','guided_practice','targeted-listening-06/aea7a4367bfc74202825f682ccd28f1a2d07e403f2dc0a602800c4e650cde237/BH-TL06-L10A_v1.mp3','aea7a4367bfc74202825f682ccd28f1a2d07e403f2dc0a602800c4e650cde237','You will hear part of a lecture about the design of queues. Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then listen to the first half of the lecture and choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D. You may pause or replay.','Signposts tell you where you are in a lecture: ''Let''s start with'', ''This leads to'', ''The first is''. Listen for them, then for the point that follows.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "What is the lecture mainly about?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "48a691fe-9224-405f-8f96-7db6772633aa", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  why queues are getting longer"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how businesses design the way people wait"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  how to reduce the number of customers"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  the history of banks and post offices"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "When was the well-known paper on waiting lines published?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "supporting_node_id": "1b02bd3d-3b53-4357-ad5b-3e31e3c5f5bd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  last term"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  in the 1960s"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  in the 1980s"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  in the 1990s"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "What does ''This leads to an important point'' introduce?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "supporting_node_id": "472c05b5-8cc0-43b8-b300-ae5b769d7769", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a criticism of single lines"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the end of the lecture"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a new example about lifts"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  the idea that how long a wait feels matters more than its real length"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Why does the lecturer describe the class poll?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "01cbe6ee-f40e-4416-bc41-4c95c67d156a", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  to show that students are bad at maths"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  to illustrate how unreliable our sense of time is"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  to present the results of a major study"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  to prove that the canteen is too slow"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "After ''several factors stand out'', which factor does the lecturer give first?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "supporting_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  uncertainty"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  fairness"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  company"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "What is ''a well-known example'' used to show?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "supporting_node_id": "472c05b5-8cc0-43b8-b300-ae5b769d7769", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  that office workers are impatient"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  that faster lifts are always better"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  that mirrors are cheap"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  that people complain less when they have something to do"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS listening exercise; not a full paper or an official IELTS recording.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-listening-targeted-focus-v1", "passage": "LEARN FIRST: signposts show the structure\n\nA Part 4 lecture is one speaker, no breaks. Lecturers help you with signposts:\n- the topic: In today''s lecture I want to look at ...\n- the first point: Let''s start with ... / First, ...\n- the next point: This leads to ... / The second factor is ...\n- an example: For example ... / A well-known example comes from ...\n- a conclusion: So the question is ... / Finally, ...\nThe answer usually comes just AFTER the signpost, not before it.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nYou hear: ''Let''s start with the shape of the queue itself.''\nQuestion: What does the lecturer discuss first? -> the shape of the queue.\nThe signpost ''Let''s start with'' tells you this is point one.\n\nNow listen and answer six questions.", "focus": "Track spoken logical relationships: use a lecturer''s signposts to follow the structure of a Part 4 talk."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('teacher_notes', '1 B, The lecturer says: "In today''s lecture I want to look at something we all experience but rarely think about: waiting in queues" and "Businesses spend a surprising amount of effort designing how we wait".
2 C, "a well-known paper on the psychology of waiting lines, published in the nineteen-eighties". ''Last term'' is when the lecturer ran the poll.
3 D, Straight after the signpost: "What matters to customers is often not the actual length of the wait, but how long it feels."
4 B, "treat it as an illustration rather than a research finding, but it does show how unreliable our sense of waiting time can be".
5 A, "The first is having nothing to do." Uncertainty is the second factor; fairness and company come in the second half.
6 D, The example is the mirrors beside the lifts: "People spent the time checking their appearance, and the complaints largely stopped, even though the lifts were no faster."
Read each explanation, then listen to that part again and hear the proof yourself.

